<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Permanent Settlement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1793">1793</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Permanent Settlement of Bengal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Permanent Settlement, or Permanent Settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, was an agreement concluded in 1793 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> administration under Charles, Earl Cornwallis, with the landlords of Bengal, fixing for good the revenue to be raised from land. Part of the Cornwallis Code, it made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a>, who under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> had collected revenue under supervision, into holders treated as landowners who paid a fixed sum in perpetuity, creating an Indian landed class that supported British authority. Introduced in Bengal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and later elsewhere, it lowered the Company's income over time, while the peasantry suffered as landlords pressed them to grow cash crops.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> received the diwani of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-buxar">Battle of Buxar</a> in 1764, it lacked administrators who knew local custom and law, and revenue was extracted with little regard for future income or welfare. After the famine of 1770, which this short-sightedness helped to cause, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a> introduced five-yearly inspections and temporary tax farmers, many of whom made off with what they could between inspections. In 1784 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger">William Pitt the Younger</a> ordered the system changed, and in 1786 the Court of Directors proposed a permanent settlement and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-cornwallis-1st-marquess-cornwallis">Charles Cornwallis</a> was sent out to reform the Company's practices.</p><p>Between 1786 and 1790 Cornwallis argued the case with Sir John Shore, who warned that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> would not believe the settlement to be permanent. A ten-year settlement of 1790 was made permanent in 1793.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The settlement was made with the existing rajas and taluqdars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, now classed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a>, whose tenure was secured on condition of paying a fixed revenue for ever, set at 89% of the rent with 11% kept by the zamindar. Payment was due before sunset on the appointed day, which gave it the name Sunset Law, and land in arrears was sold to the highest bidder. The zamindars lost their right to keep armed forces and to hold courts. Cornwallis expected that, with the state's demand fixed, they would invest in drainage, irrigation, roads and bridges, as improving landlords such as Coke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a> did in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The demand was inflexible, with no allowance for drought or flood, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> fell into arrears at once. The auctions created a market in land, bought largely by Indian officials of the Company, and historians such as Bernard S. Cohn argue that the ruling class of the countryside shifted from lineages and local chiefs to civil servants, merchants and bankers, often absentee landlords.</p><p>In 1819 Francis Rawdon-Hastings wrote that it had subjected almost all the lower classes to most grievous oppression, while in 1829 Lord Bentinck valued the body of rich proprietors it had created as loyal to British rule. The regulations lasted until the Charter Act 1833, and the landlords' power over smallholders was not reduced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> until land reform began in the 1950s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Permanent Settlement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Mughals zamindars had not owned the land, only the right to collect its revenue, but in 1793 the <b>Permanent Settlement</b> made the zamindars of Bengal, Bihar and Orissa proprietors of their land in return for a fixed annual rent.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
